      "text": "We need to compute the expression \\(3 \\oplus 1\\) using the given operation \\(a \\oplus b = 3a + 4b\\).\n\nFirst, substitute \\(a = 3\\) and \\(b = 1\\) into the expression:\n\\[\n3 \\oplus 1 = 3(3) + 4(1)\n\\]\n\nNext, perform the multiplications:\n\\[\n3(3) = 9\n\\]\n\\[\n4(1) = 4\n\\]\n\nNow, add the results of the multiplications:\n\\[\n9 + 4 = 13\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(3 \\oplus 1\\) is \\(\\boxed{13}\\).\n\nDefine $\\dfrac{n}{p_{1}+p_{2}+\\cdots+p_{n}}$ as the \"average reciprocal\" of $n$ positive numbers $p_{1}$, $p_{2}$, $\\ldots$, $p_{n}$. If it is known that the sequence $\\{a_{n}\\}$ is an arithmetic sequence, and the \"average reciprocal\" of its first $n$ terms is $n^{-2}$, then $a_{1}+a_{2016}=\\boxed{\\text{\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_}}$.\n\nSince $\\{a_{n}\\}$ is an arithmetic sequence, let the first term be $a$ and the common difference be $d$ (all terms are positive). The average reciprocal of the first $n$ terms of $\\{a_{n}\\}$ is given by:\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{a + (a+d) + \\cdots + [a + (n-1)d]} = \\frac{n}{na + d(n-1)} = \\frac{1}{a + \\frac{d}{n}} = n^{-2}\n\\]\nThis equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{a + \\frac{d}{n}} = n^{-2}\n\\]\nwhich implies:\n\\[\na + \\frac{d}{n} = n^2\n\\]\nThus, we have:\n\\[\na + \\frac{d}{n} = n^2 \\implies \\frac{d}{n} = n^2 - a \\implies d = n^3 - an\n\\]\nSince $d$ is a constant, the coefficients of $n$ and the constant term must separately form an arithmetic sequence. This implies $1 = -a$, so:\n\\[\na = -1\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nd = n^3 + 1\n\\]\nTherefore, the general term of the arithmetic sequence is:\n\\[\na_n = -1 + (n-1)(n^3 + 1) = -1 + n^3 - n^2 - 1 = n^3 - n^2 - n - 1\n\\]\nWe need to find $a_1 + a_{2016}$:\n\\[\na_1 = 1^3 - 1^2 - 1 - 1 = -2\n\\]\n\\[\na_{2016} = 2016^3 - 2016^2 - 2016 - 1\n\\]\nSince $a_{2016} = -2 - 2016 - 2016 - 1 = -4035$, we have:\n\\[\na_1 + a_{2016} = -2 + (-4035) = -4037\n\\]\nThus, the value is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-4037}\n\\]\n\nMark has to wait 3 days for his first 